zTop on a server
================

The package carries two agents. The user service (systemctl --user) serves one
person's session on a desktop. The system service, ztop-agent.service, serves a
machine nobody is logged in to, for a hub or the zTop app on another machine.

The system service is off until you turn it on. It runs as the unprivileged
ztop user, with TLS, and keeps its certificate and the hashes of its tokens in
/var/lib/ztop. Clients pin its certificate and send a token; it never accepts
signals, renice or token management, and it never advertises itself.

1. Choose the address. The agent serves 127.0.0.1 unless /etc/default/ztop-agent
   says otherwise. Bind the address of a private network on this machine, such
   as its Tailscale or WireGuard address, never a public one:

       ZTOP_AGENT_ARGS="--bind 100.64.0.10"

   Allow the agent's port (7100) only from the machines that should read it,
   with the private network's access rules or a firewall.

2. Pair the client, once per client:

       sudo /usr/lib/ztop/pair-system-agent

   It prints {"pin":"SHA256:...","token":"..."}. Give the pin and the token to
   the client, for example as one entry of a hub's agents file:

       {"name": "web-1", "target": "100.64.0.10:7100",
        "pin": "SHA256:...", "token": "..."}

   The pairing code never reaches the journal or the network: the helper pairs
   over loopback and prints only the result.

3. Turn the agent on:

       sudo systemctl enable --now ztop-agent
       journalctl -u ztop-agent

Upgrades leave a running agent on the old binary until it restarts
(sudo systemctl restart ztop-agent). Removing the package stops and disables
it; purging also deletes /var/lib/ztop, so every client has to pair again.

To revoke every client, stop the agent, delete /var/lib/ztop/ztop/tokens.json
and start it again.
